        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-jane-eyre-88509b330c80e2fb\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"The negated utterance straightforwardly presents as true Jane’s absence of loving feeling toward Mrs. Reed. Although delivered during an emotionally charged act of retaliation, it is explicitly framed as a truthful declaration, not as a relational undertaking or a reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The narration’s “Speak I must” and subsequent catharsis create limited expressive pressure, but Jane gathers her energies and formulates a deliberate declaration; the intensity and release do not establish independent E force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Jane directly tells Mrs. Reed, “I declare I do not love you,” within a face-to-face confrontation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"After Mrs. Reed depicts Jane as deceitful, Jane retaliates by contrasting the loving claim she would make if she were deceitful with what she declares to be the truth: she does not love Mrs. Reed and strongly dislikes her. The speech is impassioned and liberating for Jane, but the highlighted core remains a deliberate negative avowal rather than a commitment or reflexive outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“if I were [deceitful], I should say I loved you; but I declare I do not love you”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit contrast between deceit and declaration strongly supports truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Jane continues, “I dislike you the worst of anybody in the world except John Reed.”\",\"supports\":\"The adjacent statement corroborates the literal avowal of Jane’s hostile emotional state.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I gathered my energies and launched them in this blunt sentence”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is deliberately formulated as verbal retaliation, weighing against a predominantly reflex-like reading.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Jane feels resentment before speaking and afterwards experiences freedom and triumph, “as if an invisible bond had burst.”\",\"supports\":\"The confrontation is emotionally intense and cathartic, while this framing concerns its context and effects more than an independent expressive force of the core words.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a negative-present, explicit first-person love construction in Chapter IV of Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the classified core is an explicit present-tense denial of love.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally represents the core negative avowal. Its retaliatory purpose, emotional delivery, and cathartic effect are adequately handled as context and do not require P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"Negation changes the content of the avowed state, not its truth-conditional mode.\"}"
        }
